# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env.local for development. Set the same names in the host's
# environment when deploying. .env.local is gitignored; never commit real keys.
#
# The site works with NONE of these set. Without them it serves the defaults in
# src/config/business.ts and the request form logs instead of sending, so
# nothing breaks while you are still setting things up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Service request email -------------------------------------------------
# Without these the form still works in `next dev` (submissions are logged to
# the terminal) but WILL RETURN AN ERROR in production rather than silently
# dropping a lead.
#
#   1. Create a free account at https://resend.com
#   2. Add and verify the site's domain under Domains
#   3. Create an API key under API Keys
#
# Note: RESEND_FROM must be on a domain verified in Resend. The address leads
# are delivered TO is set in the admin panel, not here, and can be any inbox.

RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
RESEND_FROM="FMA Industries <service@fmaindustries.com>"


# --- Database (admin panel) ------------------------------------------------
# From the Supabase dashboard: Project Settings -> API.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY bypasses Row Level Security. It is read only by
# src/lib/supabase.ts, which is marked `server-only` so importing it from a
# Client Component is a build error. Never expose it with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.

SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# The next two are for TOOLING ONLY. The running site never reads them, so they
# do not need to be set on the deployment host.
#
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY lets `npm run verify:db` prove that the anon key reads
# nothing, which is the entire point of enabling RLS with no policies. It is on
# the same dashboard page.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# SUPABASE_DB_URL applies migrations with `npm run db:push`. It is a direct
# Postgres connection and contains the database password, which makes it the
# most dangerous value in this file. Project Settings -> Database -> Connection
# string -> URI, then replace [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the password you chose when
# you created the project.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres.xxxx:PASSWORD@aws-0-region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres


# --- Admin login -----------------------------------------------------------
# Generate all three values at once:
#
#   node scripts/hash-password.mjs "a long password"
#
# The plain password is never stored. Changing ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET signs him
# out everywhere, which is how you revoke access if a phone is lost.

ADMIN_USERNAME=musa
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=$2a$12$xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=generate_a_64_character_hex_string
